{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1544206025,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ0NTMxNjM1Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3072#issuecomment-445316352"},"message":"\u003e I was instructed to run `datalad save -r -F .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG .` to save changes of unsuccessful run (see #3071), but because my command produced new files a mere `save` is insufficient\n\nBased on testing, I don't think it is just new files, but rather new files within a subdataset.\n\n\u003e (might be sufficient in the post-revolution world) - some `add` needs to be done.\n\nYes, just tested and `datalad rev-save -r -F .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG .` handles it fine.\n\nI'm never able to keep add/save differences straight in my head, but I'm baffled why I chose to report `save` in the error given that I know `run` uses `add` underneath.\n\nGiven the uncertain revolution timeline, I'd say we should\n\n  * move --message-file/-F to common options and expose it in `add` as well.\n  * report `datalad add -r -F [...]` instead [*].\n\nThoughts?\n\n\n[*] Because we now have a `saver` argument,  perhaps it would be worth tailoring the message a bit more.  If we don't recognize it, just say something generic about commit the change and the presence of  the .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG file.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1544453231,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjAxNjA5MDY3Mw=="},"status":2}]}